MAN(1)                        Funny CLI Manual                        MAN(1)

NAME
    man — Display the manual for a command, because apparently
         remembering what your own software does is too much to ask.

    man — Display manuals for commands, or for man itself if you're feeling
         recursive.

SYNOPSIS
    man
    man <command>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'man' command displays documentation for commands available
    in the CLI.

    The 'man' command is your friendly neighborhood guide to all things CLI.

    It tells you what other commands do, how to use them, and occasionally how to
    make yourself a sandwich (not guaranteed).

    With no arguments, it lists every registered command and reminds
    you how to request a specific manual.

    With a command name, it loads that command's manual from the
    CLI's manuals directory and displays it through the built-in
    pager.

    In other words, 'man' is the part of the CLI that explains what
    all the other parts of the CLI are supposed to be doing.

    And naturally, if you ask 'man' about itself:

        man man

    The universe folds in on itself, and you see this manual. Brace yourself.

COMMAND LISTING
    When invoked without arguments, 'man' prints:

        Available commands:

    followed by every command returned by 'get_all_commands()'.

    It then suggests:

        Use 'man <command>' to learn more about a specific command.

    No manual is opened in this mode.

COMMAND LOOKUP
    When a command is supplied, 'man' first checks whether the name
    is an alias.

    If it is, the alias is resolved to its actual command before
    looking for the manual.

    This means:

        man ll

    can resolve to the manual for:

        ls

    assuming 'll' is registered as an alias for 'ls'.

    Because even documentation needs alias support.

MANUAL FILES
    Manuals are loaded from the CLI's 'manuals' directory.

    The command first looks for:

        <command>.txt

    If that file does not exist, it falls back to:

        <command>

    Files are read as UTF-8 text.

    The resulting manual is passed to the built-in pager with
    25 lines per page.

ERRORS
    No manual entry for '<command>'.
        The requested command does not exist.

    No manual entry for '<command>'.
        The command exists, but its manual file could not be found.

    Yes, the same message covers both situations. Documentation
    mysteries are best left mysterious.

OUTPUT
    With no command:

        Available commands:
         - ls
         - cd
         - pwd
         ...

        Use 'man <command>' to learn more about a specific command.

    With a valid command, the corresponding manual is displayed
    through the pager.

EXAMPLES
    > man
        Lists all available commands.

        Useful when you've forgotten what you built.

    > man ls
        Displays the manual for 'ls'.

        Because even listing files deserves documentation.

    > man ll
        Resolves 'll' to its underlying command and displays the
        corresponding manual.

        The alias has been exposed.

    > man python
        Displays the Python command manual.

        This may be longer than the command itself.

    > man banana
        Prints:

            No manual entry for 'banana'.

        A devastating blow to banana-based CLI administration.

    > man man
        Congratulations! You just stared into the abyss. The abyss blinked.
        You blinked back.

FUN FACTS
    - 'man' can document aliases by resolving them before lookup.
    - Manuals are stored as plain UTF-8 text files.
    - '<command>.txt' is preferred over a file without an extension.
    - Manuals are displayed through the CLI's pager.
    - Pages are displayed in chunks of 25 lines.
    - Running 'man man' displays this manual, assuming man.txt exists.
    - The command can list everything without opening a single manual.
    - This is documentation that documents the documentation system.
    - We have officially entered metadocumentation territory.
    - Using 'man man' is technically called "metadocumentation."
    - It may cause mild existential crises in inexperienced programmers.
    - Repeated use can lead to spontaneous laughter and increased CLI wisdom.

SEE ALSO
